## 1. Scope

This reference covers polyester film, that is Mylar type, sleeves for bolts and fasteners. In a flange isolation kit the sleeve is the component that breaks the electrical path through the bolt, so its fit and damage condition matter more than its thickness alone.

## 2. Construction

- Tube formed from polyester film, supplied in a range of bore and length to suit common bolt sizes.
- Wall thickness is set by the film gauge and the number of plies.
- Fitted over the bolt before the flange is closed, so the film ends up clamped between the flanges.
- Costs less than a fluoropolymer sleeve of the same size, which is why it is used across a whole kit.

## 3. Typical specification range

| Parameter | Typical value | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Material | Polyester film, Mylar type | Electrical and mechanical properties declared per film standard. |
| Form | Sleeve cut to length for a bolt size | Bore must clear the bolt and the wall must clear the hole. |
| Tolerance | Bore and wall to suit the bolt and the bolt hole | Bolt hole size varies between flange standards. |
| Quantity use | One per bolt, consumed across the kit | Cost per sleeve drives kit economics. |

Values above describe what is commonly specified for this component class. Confirm the exact figure against the datasheet for the grade being purchased before design release.

## 4. Selection and sizing

- The bolt size and the bolt hole diameter, both of which vary with the flange standard.
- The dielectric requirement of the isolation kit as a whole, since the sleeve is one element of it.
- The clamping load, because the sleeve is compressed between the flanges.
- Length: whether the sleeve is flush with the flange faces or protrudes into the gasket.
- Whether the kit is for a standard flange or a special one, which usually means a custom length.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- IEC 60674-3-2, requirements for biaxially oriented polyethylene terephthalate films for electrical purposes.
- ASTM D882, tensile properties of thin plastic sheeting.
- IEC 60684 series, flexible insulating sleeving, for sleeving requirements generally.
- UL 94, flammability where declared.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Handle the sleeves so edges are not nicked. A damaged edge at the flange face is where the dielectric break fails first.
- Keep the parts clean and dry before fitting.
- Fit the correct length for the flange stack. A sleeve that is too short leaves a metal path.
- Do not reuse a sleeve that has been removed after clamping.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Isolation resistance fails at test | Sleeve too short for the flange stack, or the bolt touching the hole | Specify length against the actual flange stack and confirm the sleeve clears the hole. |
| Film split during assembly | Edge damage or an oversized bolt relative to the bore | Match bore to the bolt and handle without nicking edges. |
| Kit cost pressure | Fluoropolymer sleeve specified where PET would meet the requirement | Specify PET where the temperature and chemical duty allow it. |
| Custom length lead time | Only standard lengths held | Confirm lengths against the flange schedule at the enquiry stage. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Flange isolation kits for pipelines and process plant.
- Galvanic corrosion isolation between dissimilar metals.
- Oil and gas, water and process piping where electrical isolation is required.
- Gasket kit manufacturers and pipeline corrosion suppliers.

